<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-logarithm-properties" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Logarithm Properties</title>

	<objectives>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				In this section, we learn how to:
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<ol>
			<li><p>.</p></li>
			<li><p>.</p></li>
			<li><p>.</p></li>
    </ol>
	</objectives>


	<subsection>
    <title>Laws of Logarithms</title>


    <p>
      Three formulas involving logarithms are collectively called the Laws of Logarithms:
    </p>

    <p>
      <principle xml:id="laws-of-logarithms">
        <title>Laws of Logarithms</title>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\log_b(MN) = \log_b(M) + \log_b(N)</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\log_b(\frac{M}{N}) = \log_b(M) - \log_b(N)</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\log_b(M^p) = p \cdot \log_b(M)</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </principle>
    </p>
    <p>
      <warning>
        <p>
          <m>\log_b(M) + \log_b(N) \not= \log_b(M + N)</m>. Instead, <m>\log_b(M) + \log_b(N) = \log_b(MN)</m>.
        </p>
      </warning>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use the log laws to expand the logarithms as much as possible: <m>\log_6\left(\frac{A^5B^3}{C^4D^2}\right)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>\log_6(A^5) + \log_6(B^3) - \log_6(C^4) - \log_6(D^2)</m>
            <m>= 5\log_6(A) + 3\log_6(B) - 4\log_6(C) - 2\log_6(D)</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>
    
    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use the log laws to expand the logarithms as much as possible: <m>\ln(\frac{A}{B^2C^5})</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            Recall that <m>\ln(z) = \log_e(z)</m>, so the laws of logarithms apply.  We have:
            <m>\ln\left(\frac{A}{B^2C^5}\right) = \ln(A) - \ln(B^{2}C^{5}) = \ln(A) - \left(2 \ln(B) + 5 \ln(C)\right) = \ln(A) - 2\ln(B) - 5\ln(C)</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>
    

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use the log laws to expand the logarithms as much as possible: <m>\ln(\sqrt[3]{\frac{A}{B^2C^5}})</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            
            <m>\ln\sqrt[3]{\frac{A}{B^2C^5}} = \ln\left(\frac{A^{1/3}}{B^{2/3}C^{5/3}}\right) = \ln(A^{1/3}) - \ln(B^{2/3}C^{5/3}) = \frac{1}{3}\ln(A) - \left(\frac{2}{3}\ln(B) + \frac{5}{3}\ln(C)\right)</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>
    

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use the log laws to expand the logarithms as much as possible: <m>\ln\sqrt{\frac{x+9}{3x+1}}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>\ln\sqrt{\frac{x+9}{3x+1}} = \ln\left(\frac{(x+9)^{1/2}}{(3x+1)^{1/2}}\right) = \ln((x+9)^{1/2}) - \ln((3x+1)^{1/2}) = \frac{1}{2}\ln(x+9) - \frac{1}{2}\ln(3x+1)</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Rewrite <m>\log_3(x) + \log_3(y) - 7\log_3(z)</m> as one logarithm
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>\log_3(x) + \log_3(y) - 7\log_3(z) = \log_3(xy) - \log_3(z^7) = \log_3\left(\frac{xy}{z^7}\right)</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

    
    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Rewrite <m>\ln(x) + \ln(y) - 7\ln(z)</m> as one logarithm
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>\ln(x) + \ln(y) - 7\ln(z) = \ln(xy) - \ln(z^7) = \ln\left(\frac{xy}{z^7}\right)</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>


  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Solving equations using Laws of Logarithms</title>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Solve the equation <m>\log_2(x-6) + \log_2(x-3) = \log_2(4)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            In <xref ref="principle-solve-logarithmic-one-to-one"/>, we saw that if <m>\log_b(c)=\log_b(d)</m>, then <m>c=d</m>. 
            We cannot use this property right away because the left side of the equation is not a single logarithm.
          </p>
          <p>
            We can use the Laws of Logarithms to combine the two logarithms on the left side into one logarithm:
            <me>\log_2((x-6)(x-3)) = \log_2(4)</me>
            Now we can use the property from <xref ref="principle-solve-logarithmic-one-to-one"/> to set the inputs of the logs on each side equal to each other:
            <me>(x-6)(x-3) = 4</me>
            Expanding the left side and moving all terms to one side gives:
            <me>x^2 - 9x + 18 = 4</me>
            Subtracting <m>4</m> on both sides,
            <me>x^2 - 9x + 14 = 0</me>
            Factoring the left side gives:
            <me>(x-7)(x-2) = 0</me>
            Applying the Zero Product Property,
            <me>x-7=0 \text{ or } x-2=0</me>
            So
            <me>x=7 \text{ or } x=2</me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Since the original equation has logarithms, we must check for extraneous solutions based on <xref ref="principle-check-log-equation"/>.
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Is <m>x=7</m> a solution? The input to the first logarithm is <m>7-6=1</m> and the input to the second logarithm is <m>7-3=4</m> and the input to the third logarithm is <m>4</m>. The inputs to all three logarithms are positive, so <m>x=7</m> is a solution.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Is <m>x=2</m> a solution? The input to the first logarithm is <m>2-6=-4</m> and the input to the second logarithm is <m>2-3=-1</m> and the input to the third logarithm is <m>4</m>. Because not all inputs are positive, <m>x=2</m> is not a solution.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>
    <p>
      
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Solve <m>\ln(2) + \ln(x+6)=\ln(10)+\ln(x-1)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            We can use the Laws of Logarithms to rewrite the left side as <m>\ln(2(x+6))</m>.
            We can also use the Laws of Logarithms to rewrite the right side as <m>\ln(10(x-1))</m>.  So we have:
            <me>\ln(2(x+6)) = \ln(10(x-1))</me>
            Since we now have a single logarithm on each side (of the same base, namely <m>e</m>), we can use the property from <xref ref="principle-solve-logarithmic-one-to-one"/> to set the inputs of the logs on each side equal to each other:
            <me>2(x+6) = 10(x-1)</me>
            Expanding both sides gives:
            <me>2x + 12 = 10x - 10</me>
            Subtracting <m>2x</m> from both sides gives:
            <me>12 = 8x - 10</me>
            Adding <m>10</m> to both sides gives:
            <me>22 = 8x</me>
            Dividing both sides by <m>8</m> gives:
            <me>x = \frac{22}{8} = \frac{11}{4}</me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Because the original equation had logarithms, we check to see if <m>x=\frac{11}{4}</m> is a solution.  
            The input to the first logarithm is <m>2</m>, the input to the second logarithm is <m>\frac{11}{4}+6 = \frac{35}{4}</m>, the input to the third logarithm is <m>10</m>, and the input to the fourth logarithm is <m>\frac{11}{4}-1 = \frac{7}{4}</m>.  
            All inputs are positive, so <m>x=\frac{11}{4}</m> is a solution.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>
    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Solve <m>\ln(x-3) + \ln(x+1) = \ln(x+7)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            We can use the Laws of Logarithms to rewrite the left side as <m>\ln((x-3)(x+1))</m>.  So we have:
            <me>\ln((x-3)(x+1)) = \ln(x+7)</me>
            Since we now have a single logarithm on each side (of the same base, namely <m>e</m>), we can use the property from <xref ref="principle-solve-logarithmic-one-to-one"/> to set the inputs of the logs on each side equal to each other:
            <me>(x-3)(x+1) = x+7</me>
            Expanding the left side gives:
            <me>x^2 - 2x - 3 = x + 7</me>
            Subtracting <m>x + 7</m> from both sides gives:
            <me>x^2 - 3x - 10 = 0</me>
            Factoring the left side gives:
            <me>(x-5)(x+2) = 0</me>
            Applying the Zero Product Property,
            <me>x-5=0 \text{ or } x+2=0</me>
            So
            <me>x=5 \text{ or } x=-2</me>
          </p>
          <p>
            We check to see if <m>x=5</m> is a solution.  The input to the first logarithm is <m>5-3=2</m>, the input to the second logarithm is <m>5+1=6</m>, and the input to the third logarithm is <m>5+7=12</m>.  All inputs are positive, so <m>x=5</m> is a solution.
          </p>
          <p>
            We check to see if <m>x=-2</m> is a solution.  The input to the first logarithm is <m>-2-3=-5</m>, the input to the second logarithm is <m>-2+1=-1</m>, and the input to the third logarithm is <m>-2+7=5</m>.  Not all inputs are positive, so <m>x=-2</m> is not a solution.
          </p>
          <p>
            So the only solution to the equation is <m>x=5</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Solve the equation <m>\log_8(-x+2)+\log_8(x+6) = \log_8(3-4x)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            We can use the Laws of Logarithms to combine the two logarithms on the left side into one logarithm:
            <me>\log_8((-x+2)(x+6)) = \log_8(3-4x)</me>
            Now we can use the property from <xref ref="principle-solve-logarithmic-one-to-one"/> to set the inputs of the logs on each side equal to each other:
            <me>(-x+2)(x+6) = 3-4x</me>
            Expanding the left side
            <me>-x^2 - 4x + 12 = 3-4x</me>
            <me>-x^2 - 4x + 12 - 3 + 4x = 0</me>
            <me>-x^2 + 9 = 0</me>
            Multipyling both sides by <m>-1</m> gives:
            <me>x^2 - 9 = 0</me>
            <me>(x-3)(x+3) = 0</me>
            So
            <me>x=3 \text{ or } x=-3</me>
          </p>
          <p>
            We have to check to ensure that all inputs to logarithms are positive.
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Is <m>x=3</m> a solution? The input to the first logarithm is <m>-3+2=-1</m>, the input to the second logarithm is <m>3+6=9</m>, and the input to the third logarithm is <m>3-4(3)=-9</m>.  Not all inputs are positive, so <m>x=3</m> is not a solution.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Is <m>x=-3</m> a solution? The input to the first logarithm is <m>-(-3)+2=5</m>, the input to the second logarithm is <m>-3+6=3</m>, and the input to the third logarithm is <m>-3-4(-3)=9</m>.  All inputs are positive, so <m>x=-3</m> is a solution.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
            So <m>x=-3</m> is the only solution to the equation.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
      In this example, one value of <m>x</m> was positive and one was not positive. The value of <m>x</m> that was positive created non-positive inputs to the logarithms, so it was not a solution.
      The value of <m>x</m> that was negative created positive inputs to the logarithms, so it was a solution.
      This example was specifically chosen to illustrate that it is not about the sign of <m>x</m> itself, but about the sign of the inputs to the logarithms.
    </p>


    <p>
      We saw in <xref ref="principle-solve-exponential-one-to-one"/> the technique that said that if <m>b^c=b^d</m>, then <m>c=d</m>.
      For example, in <xref ref="example-solve-one-to-one-exponential"/>, we started with <m>2^{7x+1} = 2^{3x-20}</m> and had <m>7x+1=3x-20</m> as our next step.
      In <xref ref="example-solve-one-to-one-exponential-change-base"/>, we started with <m>2^{10x+2} = 8^{2x-1}</m> and could not apply <xref ref="principle-solve-exponential-one-to-one"/> directly, 
      so we rewrote the <m>8</m> as <m>2^3</m>, making the equation <m>2^{10x+2} = (2^3)^{2x-1}</m> so <m>2^{10x+2} = 2^{3(2x-1)}</m>, and then we applied <xref ref="principle-solve-exponential-one-to-one"/> to et <m>10x+2=3(2x-1)</m>.
    </p> 
    <p>
      We were fortunate in <xref ref="example-solve-one-to-one-exponential-change-base"/> that even though the original equation didn't have the same base on each side, <m>8=2^3</m> so with some exponent rules, we were able to rewrite the equation so that we eventually had an equation with the same base on each side.
      However, what could we do if instead of <m>2^{7x+1} = 8^{2x-1}</m>, we had <m>2^{7x+1} = 5^{2x-1}</m>?
      In this example, the <m>5</m> cannot be rewritten as a power of <m>2</m>, so we cannot rewrite the equation so that we have the same base on each side.
      In this case, we can use logarithms to solve the equation: we use a technique of taking the logarithm of both sides of the equation, and then using the Laws of Logarithms to simplify the resulting equation.
      While we can choose any base we want for the logarithm, it will be convenient (due to less writing) to pick a natural logarithm.
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Solve <m>2^{7x+1} = 5^{2x-1}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            Apply the natural logarithm to both sides of the equation:
            <me>\ln(2^{7x+1}) = \ln(5^{2x-1})</me>
            Using the Laws of Logarithms, we can move the exponents to the front:
            <me>(7x+1)\ln(2) = (2x-1)\ln(5)</me>
            Notice the need to insert parentheses around <m>7x+1</m> and around <m>2x-1</m> due to Order of Operations.
            While a function such as <m>\ln(x)</m> does not distribute, the number <m>\ln(2)</m> does distribute over the sum <m>7x+1</m> and the number <m>\ln(5)</m>  distributes over the sum <m>2x-1</m>.
            <me>7x\ln(2) + \ln(2) = 2x\ln(5) - \ln(5)</me>
            Now we get all terms with <m>x</m> on one side and all terms without <m>x</m> on the other side:
            <me>7x\ln(2) - 2x\ln(5) = -\ln(2) - \ln(5)</me>
            Factor out <m>x</m> on the left side:
            <me>x(7\ln(2) - 2\ln(5)) = -\ln(2) - \ln(5)</me>
            Divide both sides by <m>7\ln(2) - 2\ln(5)</m>:
            <me>x = \dfrac{-\ln(2) - \ln(5)}{7\ln(2) - 2\ln(5)}</me>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Solve <m>4^{5x-6} = 7^{8x+9}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>
      <answer>
        <p>
          Apply the natural logarithm to both sides of the equation:
          <me>\ln(4^{5x-6}) = \ln(7^{8x+9})</me>
          Using the Laws of Logarithms, we can move the exponents to the front:
          <me>(5x-6)\ln(4) = (8x+9)\ln(7)</me>
          Notice the need to insert parentheses around <m>5x-6</m> and around <m>8x+9</m> due to Order of Operations.
          Distribute the number <m>\ln(4)</m>, and distribute the number  <m>\ln(7)</m>:
          <me>5x\ln(4) - 6\ln(4) = 8x\ln(7) + 9\ln(7)</me>
          Now we get all terms with <m>x</m> on one side and all terms without <m>x</m> on the other side:
          <me>5x\ln(4) - 8x\ln(7) = 9\ln(7) + 6\ln(4)</me>
          Factor out <m>x</m> on the left side:
          <me>x(5\ln(4) - 8\ln(7)) = 9\ln(7) + 6\ln(4)</me>
          Divide both sides by <m>5\ln(4) - 8\ln(7)</m>:
          <me>x = \dfrac{9\ln(7) + 6\ln(4)}{5\ln(4) - 8\ln(7)}</me>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </p>


  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Simplifying expressions</title>

    <p>
      Since <m>f(x) = b^x</m> and <m>g(x) = \log_b(x)</m> are inverse functions, we can write <xref ref="principle-compose-one-to-one"/> specifically for these two functions:
      <principle xml:id="principle-log-exp-inverse-formulas">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>b^{\log_{b}(x)} = x</m>
          </p>
          <p>
            <m>\log_b(b^x) = x</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </principle>
    </p>
    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Simplify <m>5^{\log_5(k)}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>5^{\log_5(k)}=k</m>
          </p>
          <p>
            This is a quick application of <m>b^{\log_{b}(x)} = x</m> in <xref ref="principle-log-exp-inverse-formulas"/>, where <m>b=5</m> and <m>x=k</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>
    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Simplify <m>5^{\log_5(k+6)}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            The expression is equal to <m>k+6</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>
    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Simplify <m>2^{10 \log_2(m)}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>2^{10 \log_2(m)} = 2^{\log_2(m^{10})} = m^{10}</m>
          </p>
          <p>
            In turning the first expression <m>2^{10 \log_2(m)}</m> into the second expression <m>2^{\log_2(m^{10})}</m>, we used the property <m>\log_b(M^p) = p \cdot \log_b(M)</m> from <xref ref="laws-of-logarithms"/> to rewrite the exponent <m>10\log_2(m)</m> as the exponent <m>\log_2(m^{10})</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>
    <p>

      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Simplify <m>\log_3(3^{u})</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            The expression is equal to <m>u</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is a quick application of <m>\log_b(b^x) = x</m> in <xref ref="principle-log-exp-inverse-formulas"/>, where <m>b=3</m> and <m>x=u</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>
    <p>

      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Simplify <m>\log_3(3^{u-1})</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            The expression is equal to <m>u-1</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>
    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Simplify <m>\log_3(9^{u-1})</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>\log_3(9^{u-1})=\log_3(3^2^{u-1})=\log_3(3^{2(u-1)})=2(u-1)=2u-2</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

  </subsection>


</section>